      "time": 1789585477.0073454,
      "body": "trainable params: 2,664,961 || all params: 361,972,994 || trainable%: 0.74%\nTrainable parameters: 2,664,961 / 361,972,994 (0.74%)\n\nWhat does trainable parameters mean?",
      "id": "f9b00aef-10e8-4387-9e9b-ee9f478073f2",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "id": "20f61504-d263-4511-b9fc-338e1009bb39",
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "\"Trainable\" means `requires_grad=True` \u2014 those are the only parameters PyTorch computes gradients for and the optimizer updates during `trainer.fit()`. Everything else in the 361,972,994 total still runs in the forward pass (it's part of the computation), but its weights never change; gradients are not tracked for it.\n\nIn your run: 361,972,994 \u2248 the 366M Surya backbone + your regression head. `apply_peft_lora()` freezes the entire backbone, then re-enables gradients only on:\n1. The small LoRA adapter matrices it inserts into whatever matched `target_modules`\n2. Every `head_*` module (kept trainable via PEFT's `modules_to_save`, per the `head_` naming convention)\n\nThat's your 2,664,961 (0.74%).\n\nHere's the useful part: I flagged earlier that your `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]` names don't exist in this model \u2014 Surya's attention uses a fused `attn.qkv` and `attn.proj`, not separate `q_proj`/`k_proj`/`v_proj`/`out_proj`. Your trainable count confirms it: for `fc1`+`fc2` LoRA (r=8) across all 10 blocks, the math works out to ~1,024,000 adapter params; add a head of roughly ~1,641,000 (consistent with the template's documented head size), and you land almost exactly at your 2,664,961.\n\nIf attention were actually being adapted too (i.e. `attn.qkv`/`attn.proj` matched), you'd expect ~491,520 *more* trainable params (8 attention blocks \u00d7 LoRA on qkv+proj) \u2014 which is close to the exact gap between your number and the 3,157,761 the template config documents for its full `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]` setup.\n\nSo right now your LoRA is silently only adapting the feedforward layers \u2014 attention stays completely frozen. If you want attention adapted as well, change `target_modules` to `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`.",
      "time": 1789585509.463275,
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": []
    },
    {
      "id": "abfe66e3-6374-4458-916c-75a20a288f5a",
      "time": 1789585595.1922426,
      "metadata": {
        "model": {
          "settings": {},
          "id": "sonnet"
        },
        "settings": {},
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      },
      "body": "What does parameters mean in simple terms?",
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "smildred",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "In a neural network like Surya, \"parameters\" are the numbers the model learns \u2014 mostly the weights and biases inside each layer's matrix multiplications (e.g. the `fc1`/`fc2` feedforward layers, the `attn.qkv` attention layer, etc.).\n\nThink of the model as one giant math formula: `output = f(input, weights)`. The `weights` are the parameters. Training doesn't change the *structure* of the formula (how many layers, their sizes) \u2014 it nudges these numbers so the formula's output gets closer to the correct answer.\n\nSo when you see \"361,972,994 params,\" that's the count of individual learnable numbers in the whole model (backbone + head). \"Trainable\" params, as discussed, are just the subset of those numbers actually allowed to change during this particular training run \u2014 the rest stay fixed at their pretrained values.",
      "id": "64b4dad6-ee8f-4057-89ae-27d573bbe4be",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789585601.2513547,
      "mentions": []
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789585741.1204603,
      "id": "c23703f6-8e5f-40dc-b2e0-e3a138ac0f08",
      "metadata": {
        "model": {
          "id": "sonnet",
          "settings": {}
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "settings": {}
      },
      "body": "Even more simple.",
      "sender": "smildred",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "time": 1789585744.6783216,
      "id": "43f92e05-fbcb-463a-88a8-93505993b98b",
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "Parameters are the model's knobs. Training turns some of those knobs to make the model's guesses better. 361 million knobs total, but only 2.6 million of them are unlocked to turn in your run \u2014 the rest are locked in place.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
    },
