      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "I've sent an explore agent to ground the details in the actual model code (patch counts, spectral vs long-short block ordering, checkpointing, time embedding mechanics, etc.) rather than rely on the CLAUDE.md summary alone. I'll follow up with the full explanation once it reports back.Here's the backbone architecture broken down, grounded in the actual code (not just the CLAUDE.md summary), using your `solar_event` config's values:\n\n**Patch embedding**: `img_size: 4096` / `patch_size: 16` \u2192 `(4096/16)\u00b2 = 65,536` patch tokens per timestep, and `grid_size=256` also sizes the spectral-gating FFT grid and the window-attention reshape (`embedding.py:67`, `spectformer.py:229-230`, `transformer_ls.py:98`). `in_channels: 13` = your 8 AIA + 5 HMI channels, fed straight into the Conv2d patch tokenizer.\n\n**Block structure**: `depth: 10` blocks total, `spectral_blocks: 2` \u2014 the first `spectral_blocks` are FFT-based spectral gating, the *rest* (8 here) are long-short attention. They're **not interspersed**: spectral blocks always run first, then attention blocks (`spectformer.py:243-269, 288-289`).\n\n**Attention hyperparameters** (only apply to the 8 attention blocks): `num_heads: 16`, `mlp_ratio: 4.0` (MLP hidden dim = `embed_dim*4`), `drop_rate: 0.0`. `window_size: 2` sizes local windowed attention (each token attends within a small neighborhood); `dp_rank: 4` sizes a separate global branch that compresses all tokens into 4 \"landmark\" summaries per head (`transformer_ls.py:28-59`) \u2014 this combination is what makes attention tractable at 65k tokens. `rpe: false` means no learned relative-position bias is added in the local-window branch (it's implemented but skipped when false).\n\n**learned_flow: false** \u2014 leaves this off; when true it warps the input through a learned per-pixel flow field via `F.grid_sample` before the backbone runs, which is exactly why it's incompatible with `training.deterministic: true` (no deterministic CUDA backward for grid_sample).\n\n**init_weights: false** \u2014 keeps SpectFormer's default trunc-normal/LayerNorm init rather than a specialized zero-init warm-start scheme.\n\n**checkpoint_layers: [0..9]** \u2014 all 10 blocks use gradient checkpointing (recompute activations in backward instead of storing them), i.e. maximum memory savings at the cost of extra recompute \u2014 sensible given `img_size: 4096` blows up activation memory otherwise.\n\n**ensemble: null** \u2014 deterministic single forward pass, no noise injection, no AdaLN modulation. A non-null integer `E` would replicate the batch `E`\u00d7 and inject per-block Gaussian noise for ensemble-style uncertainty.\n\n**time_embedding**: `type: linear` with `time_dim: 1` concatenates your single input timestep's channels into the patch conv (matches `time_delta_input_minutes: [0]`, a single frame). Worth knowing: `fourier` is declared in the config dataclass but **not actually implemented** in the model \u2014 selecting it hits a `NotImplementedError`. Only `linear` and `perceiver` (which needs `n_queries`) work.\n\n**nglo** (not in your YAML \u2014 derived): since `pooling: global_average` (not `class_token`), `nglo=0` \u2014 no global/CLS token is prepended into the attention blocks.\"Trainable\" means `requires_grad=True` \u2014 those are the only parameters PyTorch computes gradients for and the optimizer updates during `trainer.fit()`. Everything else in the 361,972,994 total still runs in the forward pass (it's part of the computation), but its weights never change; gradients are not tracked for it.\n\nIn your run: 361,972,994 \u2248 the 366M Surya backbone + your regression head. `apply_peft_lora()` freezes the entire backbone, then re-enables gradients only on:\n1. The small LoRA adapter matrices it inserts into whatever matched `target_modules`\n2. Every `head_*` module (kept trainable via PEFT's `modules_to_save`, per the `head_` naming convention)\n\nThat's your 2,664,961 (0.74%).\n\nHere's the useful part: I flagged earlier that your `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]` names don't exist in this model \u2014 Surya's attention uses a fused `attn.qkv` and `attn.proj`, not separate `q_proj`/`k_proj`/`v_proj`/`out_proj`. Your trainable count confirms it: for `fc1`+`fc2` LoRA (r=8) across all 10 blocks, the math works out to ~1,024,000 adapter params; add a head of roughly ~1,641,000 (consistent with the template's documented head size), and you land almost exactly at your 2,664,961.\n\nIf attention were actually being adapted too (i.e. `attn.qkv`/`attn.proj` matched), you'd expect ~491,520 *more* trainable params (8 attention blocks \u00d7 LoRA on qkv+proj) \u2014 which is close to the exact gap between your number and the 3,157,761 the template config documents for its full `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]` setup.\n\nSo right now your LoRA is silently only adapting the feedforward layers \u2014 attention stays completely frozen. If you want attention adapted as well, change `target_modules` to `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`.In a neural network like Surya, \"parameters\" are the numbers the model learns \u2014 mostly the weights and biases inside each layer's matrix multiplications (e.g. the `fc1`/`fc2` feedforward layers, the `attn.qkv` attention layer, etc.).\n\nThink of the model as one giant math formula: `output = f(input, weights)`. The `weights` are the parameters. Training doesn't change the *structure* of the formula (how many layers, their sizes) \u2014 it nudges these numbers so the formula's output gets closer to the correct answer.\n\nSo when you see \"361,972,994 params,\" that's the count of individual learnable numbers in the whole model (backbone + head). \"Trainable\" params, as discussed, are just the subset of those numbers actually allowed to change during this particular training run \u2014 the rest stay fixed at their pretrained values.Parameters are the model's knobs. Training turns some of those knobs to make the model's guesses better. 361 million knobs total, but only 2.6 million of them are unlocked to turn in your run \u2014 the rest are locked in place.A few things stack up to make that specific cell slow:\n\n**1. It's a genuinely large forward pass, run at full precision.** Your images are `4096\u00d74096\u00d713` channels, and at `patch_size: 16` that's `65,536` tokens per image, `embed_dim: 1280`, batch of 2. Every one of those tokens goes through 2 FFT-based spectral blocks (over a 256\u00d7256 grid) and 8 attention blocks (windowed + dp-rank global attention, 16 heads). That's a lot of compute even on a GPU. Critically, `cfg.dtype` is `training.dtype: float32` (config_script.yaml:192), and this manual call isn't wrapped in any autocast \u2014 so it's running in full fp32. Your actual `trainer.fit()` later uses `precision=\"bf16-mixed\"` on the Lightning Trainer, which is roughly 2-4x faster for the same forward pass. This ad hoc cell doesn't get that benefit.\n\n**2. No `torch.no_grad()`.** You're just inspecting the output shape/values here, not backpropagating, but since LoRA adapters and the head are trainable and threaded through every block up to the head, PyTorch is still building the full autograd graph for that whole path \u2014 extra memory and time you don't need for a sanity check.\n\n**3. `next(iter(train_data_loader))` itself can be slow.** Each call to `iter(...)` on a fresh DataLoader spins up new worker processes (this loader uses `multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"`, which starts slower than `fork`), and depending on cache state may re-download/re-decode NetCDF data from S3 (`s3_mode: download`, ~9s per file per the config comments). If your cache is cold, that alone can dominate the wall-clock time before the model even runs.\n\n**4. First-call overhead.** If this is the first forward pass since the kernel started, CUDA context/kernel compilation and cuDNN autotuning add a one-time cost that later calls won't pay.\n\nTo isolate which part is actually slow, you could time the two lines separately, and wrap the forward in `torch.no_grad()` for this inspection-only cell:\n\n```python\nimport time\nt0 = time.time(); batch = next(iter(train_data_loader)); print(\"data:\", time.time()-t0)\nt0 = time.time()\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    output = model.forward(batch)\nprint(\"forward:\", time.time()-t0)\n```\n\nWant me to add that timing split into the notebook so we can see where the time is actually going?"
